  "worked_solution": "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $x>0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x<0.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14228741/assets/af80eab6427f376084301c03715fd098cfe4a1c816dcd72d3e1129cfa35ff29d.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14228741/assets/a26ca79400bf30aa891543d30a052a6e8ec071ccb48fcd8c418fe0a713dad212.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
